@andokai, I performed further tests and discovered that the order of route definition matters. In my previous response, I overlooked testing the presence of a wildcard route. The reason behind this behavior is that routes are scanned from top to bottom. If the wildcard route is positioned above our desired route, such as `/example`, the `HEAD` request is directed to the wildcard route. However, this behavior is not observed with `GET` requests, as they function correctly. I encountered similar behavior when using both Express and Fastify. I will update the gist provided above to reflect these findings.

**TL;DR: Set wildcard route at the bottom most in your code (quick fix)**

gist containing both fastify and express comparison [https://gist.github.com/ashutoshdhande/4895b5ae808ed985c0c821a375425153](url)

This is the result I've got if I register the routes without `autoload`:
```shell
└── /
    ├── example (GET, HEAD)
    ├── other (GET, HEAD)
    ├── nested/
    │   ├── one (GET, HEAD)
    │   ├── two (GET)
    │   ├── something/
    │   │   └── :{id} (GET, HEAD)
    │   └── :param (GET, HEAD)
    └── another/one (GET, HEAD)
```

It seems that in `nested` context, one of them is missed, but the order matters.
If the parametrised is registered first, the static ones are not registered. But if the static ones are declared first, just one gets registered meanwhile the rest is not, but parametrised ones are still registered.

```js
const fastify = require('fastify')({
  logger: true
})

fastify.get('/example', async (request, reply) => {
  reply.type('application/json').code(200)

  puppet = await puppeteer.launch({
    headless: false
  })

  return { hello: 'world' }
})

fastify.get('/other', async (request, reply) => {
  reply.type('application/json').code(200)

  puppet = await puppeteer.launch({
    headless: false
  })

  return { hello: 'world' }
})

fastify.register(
  (instance, _opts, done) => {
    instance.get('/one', () => {})
    instance.get('/:param', () => {})
    instance.get('/two', () => {})
    instance.get('/something/:{id}', () => {})

    done();
  },
  {
    prefix: '/nested'
  }
)


fastify.register(
  (instance, _opts, done) => {
    instance.get('/one', () => {})

    done();
  },
  {
    prefix: '/another'
  }
)

fastify.addHook('onReady', async function hook () {
  console.log(fastify.printRoutes())
})

fastify.listen().then(console.log)
```

